.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt MEMFD_SECRET 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm memfd_secret
.Nd make a file in memory that only its mappings reach
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/syscall.h
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn syscall SYS_memfd_secret "unsigned int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn memfd_secret
makes an empty regular file in memory whose bytes only its shared
mappings reach, as Linux's call does, and returns a new descriptor
open on it for reading and writing.
The file has mode 0600 and the caller's effective user and group, one
link and no blocks;
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
and
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /maps
name it
.Ql /secretmem (deleted) .
It goes when the last descriptor or mapping of it goes.
.Pp
The file is used by giving it a size once, with
.Xr ftruncate 2 ,
and mapping it with
.Dv MAP_SHARED .
Then:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Xr read 2
and
.Xr write 2
of it fail with
.Er EINVAL ;
a truncation once its size is not 0 fails with
.Er EINVAL ;
.Xr fallocate 2
fails with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ;
and
.Xr fcntl 2 Ns 's
seal commands fail with
.Er EINVAL .
.It
A private mapping of it fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
and one with
.Dv PROT_EXEC
with
.Er EPERM ;
.Xr mprotect 2
refuses execute with
.Er EACCES .
A touch of a page past the size raises
.Dv SIGBUS .
.It
A page is made at its first touch and counted for the caller's real
user, against the soft
.Dv RLIMIT_MEMLOCK
of the process that touches it, with no exception for privilege; a touch
past the limit raises
.Dv SIGBUS .
The count goes down when the file goes.
.Xr mlock 2
and
.Xr mlock2 2
pass the mapping over, neither locking it nor counting it in
.Ql VmLck ,
and then fail with
.Er ENOMEM ,
since no page of it can be brought in; the rest of the range is locked.
.Xr munlock 2
and
.Xr mlockall 2
pass it over and succeed.
.Dv MAP_POPULATE
and
.Dv MAP_LOCKED
bring none of its pages in.
.It
No other process or device reaches its pages:
.Xr ptrace 2
and
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /mem
fail with
.Er EIO ,
.Xr process_vm_readv 2
and
.Xr process_vm_writev 2
with
.Er EFAULT ,
a device that would pin the pages fails, a virtual machine of
.Pa /dev/kvm
cannot use them as its memory, and a core file leaves the mapping out.
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /smaps
gives the mapping the flag
.Ql dd .
.El
.Pp
Linux also takes each page out of the kernel's own mapping of memory,
so that a fault of the kernel cannot read it; Rach2's kernel keeps its
mapping of every page.
The call is on both kernels, as Linux's is on i386 and x86-64.
.Pp
.Fa flags
is 0 or
.Dv O_CLOEXEC ,
which sets
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
on the descriptor.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The new descriptor.
On failure -1 is returned with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EMFILE]"
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fa flags
has a bit other than
.Dv O_CLOEXEC .
.It Bq Er EMFILE
The process has no free descriptor below its limit.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel heap has no room for the file or its description.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr brk 2 ,
.Xr cachestat 2 ,
.Xr memfd_create 2
.Sh STANDARDS
Linux 5.14 added the call.
